None\\n        else:\\n            error_line = len(lines)\\n    return {\\\"valid\\\": valid, \\\"error_line\\\": error_line, \\\"trajectory\\\": trajectory}\\n\"}","reasoning_content":"We need to interpret the task. The task is AGENT-RA-01. The payload contains \"lines\" array of strings. We need to return an object with \"valid\", \"error_line\", and \"trajectory\". The trajectory is an array of objects with \"type\" and \"content\". Likely we need to parse the lines as a trajectory of agent steps. The lines seem to be like \"Thought: ...\", \"Action: ...\", \"Observation: ...\", \"Final: ...\". The task might be to validate if the trajectory follows a correct pattern. The example cases all have same lines. But we need to infer the rule.\n\nGiven the task name \"AGENT-RA-01\", perhaps it's about verifying a ReAct agent trajectory? The lines are strings like \"Thought: inspect\", \"Action: lookup\", \"Observation: found\", \"Thought: answer\", \"Final: done\". The output must have \"valid\" boolean, \"error_line\" null or integer (line number where error occurs?), and \"trajectory\" array of steps.\n\nI need to parse each line into a trajectory object. The \"type\" could be derived from the prefix before colon. For example, \"Thought: inspect\" -> type \"thought\", content \"inspect\". But the schema says \"type\" is string, no restrictions. Also \"content\" is string. So we can map.\n\nBut what determines \"valid\"? Possibly the trajectory must follow alternating pattern: thought -> action -> observation -> thought -> ... and end with Final. Also each step must have proper content? For these examples, all lines are valid? So valid=true, error_line=null.\n\nBut we need to ensure our solve handles any possible lines. The public fixtures only show these three identical cases, but the task says \"The attached public fixtures define input shapes only.\" So the actual test cases may have different lines.\n\nWe need to design a robust parser. The lines may have arbitrar