even if invalid? But if invalid, we might still parse them. But we need to decide.\n\nLet's think typical ReAct validation: The trajectory should follow the pattern. If any line is malformed (no colon) or type not in allowed set, or sequence wrong, then set valid false and error_line to the first offending line number.\n\nIn the examples, all lines are valid. So our function should return valid=true, error_line=null, trajectory with 5 objects: {type:\"Thought\", content:\"inspect\"}, {type:\"Action\", content:\"lookup\"}, {type:\"Observation\", content:\"found\"}, {type:\"Thought\", content:\"answer\"}, {type:\"Final\", content:\"done\"}\n\nNow, we need to code this. Use Python standard library only. Implementation:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    lines = case.get(\"lines\", [])\n    trajectory = []\n    valid = True\n    error_line = None\n    # Define allowed types and expected sequence\n    # The sequence must be: after Intent? Actually ReAct: Thought -> Action -> Observation -> Thought -> ... -> Final.\n    # The cycle: Thought, Action, Observation (with no mandatory sequence for the first? Usually start with Thought.)\n    # After Observation, next must be either Thought or Final? In typical, after Observation you can go to Thought or Final if final answer.\n    # But here we have explicit Final marker.\n    # Let's define allowed types set: {\"Thought\", \"Action\", \"Observation\", \"Final\"}\n    allowed_types = {\"Thought\", \"Action\", \"Observation\", \"Final\"}\n    # Define expected next type based on current type.\n    # Initialize expected_next = \"Thought\" (first line must be Thought)\n    expected_next = \"Thought\"\n    for i, line in enumerate(lines, start=1):\n        # Parse line: split on first \": \"\n        if \": \" not in line:\n            # invalid line\n            valid = False\n            error_line = i\n            # Still parse? We'll break or continue? I'll break to set error_line to first invalid.\n            # But also we need to add