to not overwrite error_line.\n            # check if type matches expected_next\n            if type_ != expected_next:\n                valid = False\n                error_line = i\n                break\n            # update expected_next\n            if type_ == \"Thought\":\n                expected_next = \"Action\"\n            elif type_ == \"Action\":\n                expected_next = \"Observation\"\n            elif type_ == \"Observation\":\n                # after observation, next can be Thought or Final\n                # but Final can only appear once at the end. We'll handle that.\n                # For now, we set expected_next to either \"Thought\" or \"Final\". But we need to decide dynamically.\n                # Actually after observation, the next should be Thought (to continue) or Final (to end).\n                # Since we are parsing sequentially, we cannot know if next is final until we see it.\n                # So we set expected_next to None (any allowed? But then we need to check.\n                # Better: after Observation, expected_next can be \"Thought\" or \"Final\". So we set a set.\n                expected_next = {\"Thought\", \"Final\"}\n            elif type_ == \"Final\":\n                # after final, no more steps expected. So we set expected_next to None.\n                expected_next = None\n        else:\n            # already invalid, we can break or continue but we need to maintain trajectory for rest? Possibly break early.\n            # Once invalid, we still need to include all lines in trajectory? The requirement: trajectory array contains all steps? Not clear.\n            # The schema says trajectory is array of objects. It doesn't say it must only contain valid steps.\n            # I'll break to stop processing further, but then trajectory will only include up to the invalid line.\n            # However the examples have all valid, so not an issue.\n            break\n    # After loop, if we broke due to invalid, we have trajectory partial.\n    # Also need to check if the last expected_next is satisfied: if valid and expected_next is not None, then sequence incomplete.\n    # For example, if we ended on Observation without Final, that's invalid.\n    # So after loop, if valid and expected_next is not None and expected_next != {\"Thought\",\"Final\"}? Actually need to check.\n    # Better to check at end: if valid and (expected_next is not None) then invalid. Because after the last step, we expect nothing (if ended with Final) or need to continue? Actually if last step is Observation, then next should be Thought or Final, but we have no next, so invalid (missing Termination). Similarly if last step is Action, missing Observation. If last step is Thought, missing Action.\n    # So after processing all lines, if valid and expected_next is not None and not (expected_next == {\"Thought\",\"Final\"} and possible? Actually if expected_next is a set, it means we are in state after Observation. If we have no more lines, that's incomplete -> invalid. So we set valid=False and set error_line = len(lines) + 1? But the problem says error_line is integer (null if valid). For incomplete, maybe error_line is the line after last? Not defined. I'll set error_line = len(lines) (the last line) or maybe null? Better to set error_line = None because the error is not on a specific line but on missing final step? However we need to assign an integer. The spec says \"error_line\" can be integer or null. In many such tasks, they set error_line to the line number where the pattern breaks, including missing. For missing final, it could be the last line plus one, but that might be out of range. I'll set it to the last line index (len(lines)) as the location of the error? Or set to None? I'll decide to set error_line = len(lines) (the last line number) if the sequence is incomplete and no specific line caused it. But the schema doesn't specify, so I'll be safe: if valid is True but expected_next is