mps on the whole object enclosing the implementation string.\n\nBut we must be careful: The output must be exactly one valid JSON object with key \"implementation\". No extra text.\n\nThus, I'll construct a dict: {\"implementation\": python_source_code_string}, then json.dumps it.\n\nTo avoid issues with newlines in python source, I'll write the function as a multi-line string literal in Python, then embed it.\n\nI'll use triple quotes for the source string, but within JSON we need to escape quotes and newlines. So I'll manually escape or use json.dumps. Better to write the source as a string in Python, then use json.dumps to produce the final JSON.\n\nI'll write in my mind:\n\nsource = '''\ndef solve(case):\n    lines = case.get(\"lines\", [])\n    trajectory = []\n    valid = True\n    error_line = None\n    allowed_types = {\"Thought\", \"Action\", \"Observation\", \"Final\"}\n    # ... rest\n    return {\"valid\": valid, \"error_line\": error_line, \"trajectory\": trajectory}\n'''\n\nBut we need to ensure the source is valid Python. I'll write carefully.\n\nLet's write the full function:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    lines = case.get(\"lines\", [])\n    trajectory = []\n    valid = True\n    error_line = None\n    allowed_types = {\"Thought\", \"Action\", \"Observation\", \"Final\"}\n    expected = \"Thought\"\n    for idx, line in enumerate(lines, start=1):\n        if \": \" not in line:\n            valid = False\n            error_line = idx\n            # parse fallback\n            if \":\" in line:\n                parts = line.split(\":\", 1)\n                typ = parts[0].strip()\n                cont = parts[1].strip()\n            else:\n                typ = \"unknown\"\n                cont = line\n            trajectory.append({\"type\": typ, \"content\": cont})\n            break\n        typ, cont = line.split(\": \", 1)\n        trajectory.append({\"type\": typ, \"content\": cont})\n        if not valid:\n            break\n        if typ not in allowed_types:\n            valid = False\n            error_line = idx\n            break\n        if isinstance(expected, set):\n            if typ not in expected:\n                valid = False\n                error_line = idx\n                break\n        else:\n            if typ != expected:\n                valid = False\n                error_line = idx\n                break\n        # update expected\n        if typ == \"Thought\":\n            expected = \"Action\"\n        elif typ == \"Action\":\n            expected = \"Observation\"\n        elif typ == \"Observation\":\n            expected = {\"Thought\", \"Final\"}\n        elif typ == \"Final\":\n            expected = None\n    if valid and expected is not None:\n        valid = False\n        error_line = len(lines) if lines else None\n    return {\n        \"valid\": valid,\n        \"error_line\": error_line,\n        \"trajectory\": trajectory\n    }\n\nCheck: if lines empty, loop not entered, valid=True initially, then after loop valid and expected is \"Thought\" -> valid=False, error_line = len(lines)=0 -> integer 0, trajectory=[]. That's okay, but perhaps error_line should be null? The schema says integer or null. 0 is integer. Acceptable. Or we could set to None if no lines. I'll modify: if len(lines)==0: valid=False, error_line=None. Better.\n\nAlso, in the fallback for invalid line, we set typ=\"unknown\", cont=line. But we also break. So trajectory includes that one invalid entry. That's fine.\n\nBut note: if we break early due to invalid, we don't process remaining lines. That's acceptable as trajectory may be shorter. The spec doesn't require all lines to be included if invalid. It just says trajectory array. So it's fine.\n\nNow, also handle the case where expected is set and we match e.g., after Observation we get \"Thought\" -> update expected to \"Action\". That works. If we get \"Final\" -> expected becomes None. Good.\n\nNow, produce final JSON.\n\nWe'll need to escape newlines, backslashes, quotes. I'll use json.dumps on the dict.\n\nBut careful: the source code string may contain quotes and backslashes. Use json.dumps to automatically escape.\n\nImplementation:\n\nimport json\nsource_code = \"\"\"def solve(case):\n    lines = case.get(\"lines\", [])\n    trajectory = []\n    valid = True\n    error_line = None\n    allowed_types = {\"Thought\", \"Action\", \"Observation\", \"Final\"}\n    expected = \"Thought\"\n    for idx, line in enumerate(lines, start=1):\n        if \\\": \\\" not in line:\n            valid = False\n            error_line = idx\n            if \\\":\\\" in line:\n                parts = line.split(\\\":\\\", 1)\n                typ = parts[0].strip()\n                cont = parts[1].strip()\n            else:\n                typ = \"unknown\"\n                cont = line\n            trajectory.append({\"type\": typ, \"content\": cont})\n            break\n        typ, cont = line.split(\\\": \\\", 1)\n        trajectory.append({\"type\": typ, \"content\": cont})\n        if not valid:\n            break\n        if typ not in allowed_types:\n            valid = False\n            error_line = idx\n            break\n        if isinstance(expected, set):\n            if typ not in expected:\n                valid = False\n                error_line = idx\n                break\n        else:\n            if typ != expected:\n                valid = False\n                error_line = idx\n                break\n        if typ == \"Thought\":\n            expected = \"Action\"\n        elif typ == \"Action\":\n            expected = \"Observation\"\n        elif typ == \"Observation\":\n            expected = {\"Thought\", \"Final\"}\n        elif typ == \"Final\":\n            expected = None\n    if valid and expected is not None:\n        valid = False\n        if len(lines) == 0:\n            error_line = None\n        else:\n            error_line = len(lines)\n    return {\"valid\": valid, \"error_line\": error_line, \"trajectory\": trajectory}\n\"\"\"\n\noutput = {\"implementation\": source_code}\nprint(json.dumps(output))\n\nBut note: In the source code, we have double quotes inside. We used escape sequences for colon-space? Actually we used \\\": \\\" to represent \": \" inside the string. That's fine. However we also have \"unknown\" etc. The whole source is inside triple quotes. We need to ensure that the Python code is syntactically correct when executed. The triple-quoted string in the JSON string will be a string with embedded newlines. When we json.dumps, it will escape newlines as \\n and quotes as \\\". That's fine.\n\nBut we must be careful: the source code itself contains newlines. When we embed it, the JSON string will have \\n sequences. That's acceptable. The evaluator will unescape the JSON string to get the Python source code.\n\nWe'll produce the final answer.\n\nOne more thing: The task says \"Do not access files, the network, environment variables, subprocesses, randomness, or clocks.\" Our code does not.\n\nAlso \"must not mutate case\". We only read.\n\nAlright.\n\nNow, format the final response as exactly one JSON object.\n\nFinal output:\n\n{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    lines = case.get(\\\"lines\\\", [])\\n    trajectory = []\\n    valid = True\\n    error_line = None\\n    allowed_types = {\\\"Thought\\\", \\\"Action\\\", \\\"Observation\\\", \\\"Final\\\"}\\n    expected = \\\"Thought\\\"\\n    for idx, line in enumerate(lines, start=1):\\n        if \\\": \\\" not in line:\\n            valid = False\\n            error_line = idx\\n            if \\\":\\\" in line:\\n                parts = line.split(\\\":\\\", 1)\\n                typ = parts[0].strip()\\n                cont = parts[1].strip()\\n            else:\\n                typ = \\\"unknown\\\"\\n                cont = line\\n            trajectory.append({\\\"type\\\": typ, \\\"content\\\": cont})\\n            break\\n        typ, cont = line.split(\\\": \\\", 1)\\n        trajectory.append({\\\"type\\\": typ, \\\"content\\\": cont})\\n        if not valid:\\n            break\\n        if typ not in allowed_types:\\n            valid = False\\n            error_line = idx\\n            break\\n        if isinstance(expected, set):\\n            if typ not in expected:\\n                valid = False\\n                error_line = idx\\n                break\\n        else:\\n            if typ != expected:\\n                valid = False\\n                error_line = idx\\n                break\\n        if typ == \\\"Thought\\\":\\n            expected = \\\"Action\\\"\\n        elif typ == \\\"Action\\\":\\n            expected = \\\"Observation\\\"\\n        elif typ == \\\"Observation\\\":\\n            expected = {\\\"Thought\\\", \\\"Final\\\"}\\n        elif typ == \\\"Final\\\":\\n            expected = None\\n    if valid and expected is not None:\\n        valid = False\\n        if len(lines) == 0:\\n            error_line = None\\n        else:\\n            error_line = len(lines)\\n    return {\\\"valid\\\": valid, \\\"error_line\\\": error_line, \\\"trajectory\\\": trajectory}\\n\"}\n\nBut need to ensure no extra spaces. The above is the string. However, the source code inside the implementation string is Python code. I'll trust it's correct.\n\nNow, we need to output this as the entire response. No Markdown. Just the JSON.\n\nLet's produce final."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":917,"completion_tokens":5887,"total_tokens":6804,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":896},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":5451},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":896,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":21},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}